//? Title: Cannon's Algorithm: Actual Skew, Block Ownership and Rounding
//? Subtitle: A checked sequential torus simulation, the original products and explicit work counts
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Cannon's schedule makes matching matrix blocks meet by skewing rows and columns,
then cyclically shifting them. We follow the actual native buffers through the
original hand-checkable 2-by-2 product, generalize to square blocks on a smaller
logical processor grid, and retain the original random 7-by-7 comparison plot.
Datamunge now records transported source-block ids, per-round partial products
and numeric-loop work. A cancellation example corrects the old report's claim
of bit-for-bit equality: the schedule computes the matrix product in exact
arithmetic, but reorders floating-point additions. All execution here is sequential;
logical block shifts are not measurements of network traffic or parallel speedup.
)

\toc

> The original 2-by-2 product: inspect both real rounds

Let A=[1,2;3,4] and B=[5,6;7,8]. A two-by-two logical grid initially
assigns one scalar from each matrix to each cell. Skew A row i left by i
and B column j up by j, with wrap-around. The first products are 1*5,
2*8, 4*7 and 3*6, giving partial C=[5,16;28,18]. The next shift
moves A left and B up one cell. The second products complete
C=[19,22;43,50]. Each table entry below uses a carried source id and
an actual partial-output snapshot from the shared multiplication kernel.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cannon_rounds.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<double> A{1,2,3,4},B{5,6,7,8};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::cannon_matmul_trace(A,B,2,2);
  if(trace.output!=std::vector<double>{19,22,43,50}) return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1200);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=3.9;node.height=2.2;node.font_size=17;
  std::print("| round | cell (i,j) | original A id | original B id | common k | A value | B value | partial C |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& round:trace.rounds) {
    const double offset=static_cast<double>(round.step)*11;
    diagram.text({offset+2.4,2.1},std::format("round {}: actual held values and partial C",round.step),20);
    for(std::size_t i=0;i<2;++i) for(std::size_t j=0;j<2;++j) {
      const auto cell=i*2+j,aid=round.a_sources[cell],bid=round.b_sources[cell];
      if(aid%2!=bid/2) return 1;
      std::print("| {} | ({},{}) | {} | {} | {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |\n",round.step,i,j,aid,bid,aid%2,A[aid],B[bid],round.partial[cell]);
      node.fill=round.step?dm::RGB{220,252,231}:dm::RGB{239,246,255};
      diagram.node(std::format("cell_{}_{}",round.step,cell),{offset+static_cast<double>(j)*4.8,-static_cast<double>(i)*3.1},
          std::format("cell ({},{})   k={}\nA={}   B={}\npartial C={}",i,j,aid%2,A[aid],B[bid],round.partial[cell]),node);
    }
  }
  diagram.text({7.9,-5.1},"between rounds: A shifts left; B shifts up; both wrap around",19)
      .save_svg("build/cannon_rounds.svg");
  std::print("\nProduct: {}. Actual scalar multiply-adds: {}; skew copies: {}; shift copies: {}.\n",
      trace.output,trace.work.multiply_adds,trace.work.skew_copies,trace.work.shift_copies);
  std::print("Final closing shift restores skewed source ids: A {}, B {}.\n",trace.final_a_sources,trace.final_b_sources);
}
```
