\image(figures/cannon_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Arithmetic remains fixed at 884,736 scalar multiply-adds. Numeric-copy volume grows linearly with mesh side; the number of block updates grows cubically. Counts refer to this sequential implementation.)
\alttext(On logarithmic axes, scalar arithmetic is horizontal, copied values have slope one, and block updates have slope three.)

These copies include self-copies at q=1, where no off-cell communication would
be necessary. Counting assigned values is not the same as counting messages,
network hops or communication rounds. Nor does a sequential count establish
parallel speedup. The conceptual distributed benefit is that each multiplication
round needs only cyclic neighbour exchanges once data is aligned; toroidal
wrap-around is part of that logical topology. Actual performance also depends
on placement, routing, synchronization, message startup and local matrix kernels.

> Retain the original random 7-by-7 comparison, without claiming exact equality

The experiment still uses seven-by-seven matrices with entries in [-5,5) and
seed eleven. It now calls native runif(), whose SplitMix64 stream differs from
the old report's std::mt19937 draws, so old saved numbers are not reused. The
reference is the library's DenseMatrix product, an ascending-inner-index loop;
the report does not implement another multiplication algorithm locally. The
plot compares all 49 entries. Small absolute discrepancies are expected when
the cyclic order differs, even if the points visually lie on the identity line.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cannon.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::set_seed(11);constexpr int n=7;
  const auto A=dm::runif(-5,5,n*n).values(),B=dm::runif(-5,5,n*n).values();
  const dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> left(n,n,A),right(n,n,B);
  const auto standard=left*right;
  const auto cannon=dm::algorithms::cannon_matmul(A,B,n);
  double maximum=0;std::size_t unequal=0;
  for(std::size_t e=0;e<cannon.size();++e) {
    maximum=std::max(maximum,std::abs(cannon[e]-standard.data()[e]));
    unequal+=cannon[e]!=standard.data()[e];
  }
  if(maximum>1e-10 || cannon.size()!=49) return 1;
  std::print("Random 7-by-7: {} entries; unequal floating-point values: {}; maximum absolute difference: {:.17g}.\n",
      cannon.size(),unequal,maximum);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().points(standard.data(),cannon,"49 entries",{37,99,235},5)
      .abline(0,1,{148,163,184},1.5).title("Random 7-by-7: agreement to floating-point precision")
      .x_label("native ascending-k product entry").y_label("Cannon product entry")
      .save_svg("build/cannon.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Random 7-by-7: 49 entries; unequal floating-point values: 26; maximum absolute difference: 1.4210854715202004e-14.
\image(figures/cannon.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original comparison experiment, regenerated with native seeded draws. The printed error and unequal-value count describe agreement more precisely than the scatter plot.)
\alttext(All forty-nine Cannon entries lie visually on the line of equality with the standard native matrix product, while the text reports the small floating-point discrepancy.)

Exact-arithmetic equivalence is not a bitwise floating-point identity. At a
specific output entry take the terms 10^16, -10^16 and 1. Ascending order
first cancels the large pair, then adds one. The cyclic order -10^16, 1,
10^16 loses the unit before the large values cancel. The following native
one-cell and three-by-three-cell runs realize these two orders.

> Cancellation, budgets and checked dimensions

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<double> A{1e16,-1e16,1,0,0,0,0,0,0},B{0,1,0,0,1,0,0,1,0};
  const auto ascending=dm::algorithms::cannon_block_matmul(A,B,3,1);
  const auto cyclic=dm::algorithms::cannon_matmul_trace(A,B,3,3);
  if(ascending[1]!=1 || cyclic.output[1]!=0) return 1;
  std::print("Cancellation at C(0,1): one-cell ascending-k = {}; scalar-cell cyclic = {}.\n",ascending[1],cyclic.output[1]);
  std::print("Actual inner-index order:");
  for(const auto& round:cyclic.rounds) std::print(" {}",round.a_sources[1]%3);
  std::print(".\n");
  bool budget=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::cannon_matmul_trace({1,2,3,4},{5,6,7,8},2,2,7);}
  catch(const std::length_error&) {budget=true;}
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::cannon_matmul_trace({1,2,3,4},{5,6,7,8},2,2,8);
  bool shape=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::cannon_matmul({1},{1},2);}
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {shape=true;}
  bool divisor=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::cannon_work(4,3);}
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {divisor=true;}
  bool overflow=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::cannon_work(std::numeric_limits<int>::max(),1);}
  catch(const std::length_error&) {overflow=true;}
  if(!budget || !shape || !divisor || !overflow || !dm::algorithms::cannon_matmul({}, {},0).empty()) return 1;
  std::print("One-short block-update budget rejected: {}; exact budget performed {} updates.\n",budget,exact.work.block_updates);
  std::print("Bad input length rejected: {}; nondividing mesh rejected: {}; count overflow rejected before allocation: {}.\n",shape,divisor,overflow);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Cancellation at C(0,1): one-cell ascending-k = 1; scalar-cell cyclic = 0.
Actual inner-index order: 1 2 0.
One-short block-update budget rejected: true; exact budget performed 8 updates.
Bad input length rejected: true; nondividing mesh rejected: true; count overflow rejected before allocation: true.
// result_end

The empty product has n=q=0 and empty inputs. Otherwise dimensions are
nonnegative, q is a positive divisor of n, and each input has exactly n*n
values. Shape and loop-count products use checked size_t arithmetic. Budgets
bound q*q*q block updates and are checked before buffer allocation; no partial
result is returned. The original API keeps its signature and scalar schedule.
Input vectors are never changed and may alias. Ordinary double arithmetic
propagates NaN, infinity and numeric overflow; there is no finite-input or
numerical-stability guarantee hidden in dimension validation.

Ordinary simulation uses O(n*n) temporary numeric storage and
O(n*n*n+q*n*n) sequential work. The trace additionally owns q partial matrices
and q pairs of q*q source-id arrays, using O(q*(n*n+q*q)) storage. This is
a teaching/debugging trace, so large runs should normally use the untraced API.
No real dataset is needed to demonstrate this algebraic schedule; integer,
random bounded and adversarial cancellation matrices expose its relevant behavior.

Native tests independently form integer products, derive source-id permutations
from the schedule invariant, and reconstruct every recorded partial from original
blocks. They cover 6,561 exhaustive signed 2-by-2 pairs at both mesh sizes,
1,000 larger blocked cases, identity/zero/aliased inputs, budgets, exact copy
counts, ownership, malformed shapes and overflow. Random floating products
are also compared against long-double dot products with a magnitude-scaled
rounding allowance, alongside the original sparse-linear-algebra regression tests.

> References

- L. E. Cannon (1969). [A cellular computer to implement the Kalman filter algorithm](https://scholarworks.montana.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/643669d9-055d-4345-a467-41ad612599c8/content). PhD thesis, Montana State University. Original cellular-computer work.
- J. Demmel. [CS267: Cannon's algorithm on a 2D mesh](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~demmel/cs267/lecture11/lecture11.html). Block partitioning, initial alignment and cyclic multiplication schedule.
